Uber Freight — Unauthorized Access / Extortion Investigation — VERY HIGH priority. Uber Freight confirmed unauthorized access to part of its systems and repositories while saying operations remain unaffected. The investigation is ongoing, which gives you unresolved questions around initial access, repositories reached, data exposure, identity compromise, and the broader extortion campaign.
HCLTech — Hacker Breach Claim vs. Corporate Denial — VERY HIGH priority. A hacker group reportedly claimed access to employee data, while HCLTech says its investigation found no evidence its systems were compromised. This is almost tailor-made for your SecurityReceipts methodology: claim → denial → evidence → what can actually be established.
Taiwan Government — AI-Assisted Government Intrusion — EXTREME technical value. Taiwan reported an unusual AI-assisted attack involving government targets; reporting says more than 85 government accounts and thousands of personnel records were compromised, with later targeting involving nuclear-safety and energy organizations. Attribution remains an important area of uncertainty. This could become an exceptional technical Lessons piece.
Frontier Airlines — Breach + Disclosure Timeline Dispute — HIGH accountability value. Multiple lawsuits now allege inadequate security and delayed notification following attacks involving sensitive passenger/employee information. Don't treat lawsuit allegations as established facts—the interesting deep dive is comparing the alleged timeline against disclosures, notices, technical evidence and subsequent filings.
Craneware — Healthcare Data Environment Intrusion — HIGH priority. Craneware disclosed unauthorized access to part of its data environment, activated incident response, brought in external forensic specialists and notified regulators/law enforcement. It reported containment and no remaining indicators of compromise, but the disclosure leaves substantial technical questions worth tracking.
EY — Third-Party Support-System Breach — HIGH supply-chain value. EY has been notifying customers after compromise of a third-party support-ticket system. Public reporting indicates support-ticket attachments may have contained client tax information, while important questions such as total population affected and the identity of the third-party provider reportedly remain unresolved publicly.
WestJet — Post-Breach Security Controls / Regulatory Follow-Through — This one gives you something different from ordinary breach coverage. Canada's privacy regulator documented additional commitments, including an independent external security assessment. That means you can track what changed after the incident, rather than stopping at "WestJet got breached."
Glendale Community College — ShinyHunters / Student Records — Useful because different sources provide overlapping but not identical pictures of the exposed information. The college reported potentially copied student educational records, while Have I Been Pwned subsequently listed 793,925 unique email addresses associated with a verified dataset. That's exactly where your evidence-grading model becomes valuable.
